---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import InflationCalculator from '@components/islands/InflationCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('finance', 'inflation-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'What is inflation?', answer: 'Inflation is a general, sustained rise in prices across an economy, which means each unit of money buys a little less over time. It is usually expressed as an annual percentage — 3% inflation means a basket of goods that cost $100 last year costs about $103 this year.' },
  { question: 'What causes inflation?', answer: 'Broadly three forces: demand-pull (more spending chasing the same goods), cost-push (rising costs of labour, energy or materials pushing prices up), and monetary expansion (the money supply growing faster than the economy). Most real-world inflation is a mix of all three.' },
  { question: 'How is the inflation rate calculated?', answer: 'Statistical agencies track the price of a fixed "basket" of goods and services — the Consumer Price Index (CPI). The inflation rate is the percentage change in that index: (new CPI − old CPI) ÷ old CPI × 100. This calculator applies a constant rate you choose over the period.' },
  { question: 'Why does inflation matter for savings?', answer: 'Money that does not grow at least as fast as inflation loses buying power. At 3% inflation, cash left idle loses roughly a quarter of its value in a decade — which is why investing to outpace inflation, rather than holding only cash, matters over the long run.' },
  { question: 'What inflation rate should I use?', answer: 'Long-run inflation has averaged around 2–3% per year in many developed economies, though it varies year to year and spiked well above that in some recent years. Use a rate that reflects your expectations or your central bank\'s stated target (often 2%).' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} presentation="task-first" intro="See how inflation erodes the value of money over time — what a sum today will cost in the future, and what future money is worth now." faqs={faqs} reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-23' }}>
  <InflationCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter an <strong>amount</strong> of money — a price today, or a sum you expect to receive later.</li>
      <li>Set an expected <strong>annual inflation rate</strong> as a percentage.</li>
      <li>Choose the number of <strong>years</strong> to project over.</li>
      <li>Read both directions at once: what that amount will <strong>cost in the future</strong>, and what future money is <strong>worth in today's dollars</strong>, plus the total price change over the span.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What inflation is, and what causes it</h2>
    <p>
      Inflation is a steady rise in the general price level, so each dollar buys a little less
      each year. It comes from three broad sources: <strong>demand-pull</strong> (spending
      outpacing supply), <strong>cost-push</strong> (rising wages, energy or materials), and
      <strong>monetary</strong> growth (more money in circulation). A little inflation is
      normal and even targeted by central banks; the problem is what it does to money left
      standing still.
    </p>

    <h2>The formula</h2>
    <p>
      Inflation compounds, so a constant rate is applied year on year. The tool builds a growth
      factor and uses it in both directions:
    </p>
    <p><strong>Future cost = amount × (1 + r)<sup>t</sup></strong></p>
    <p><strong>Today's value = amount ÷ (1 + r)<sup>t</sup></strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>amount</strong> — the sum you entered</li>
      <li><strong>r</strong> — the annual inflation rate as a decimal (3% = 0.03)</li>
      <li><strong>t</strong> — the number of years</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The two are mirror images: multiplying by the factor projects a price forward, dividing by
      it discounts future money back to today. The calculator also reports the total percentage
      rise in prices across the whole period.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Suppose prices rise a steady <strong>3%</strong> a year. Something that costs
      <strong>$100 today</strong> will cost 100 × 1.03<sup>10</sup> ≈ <strong>$134.39 in ten
      years</strong>. Flip it around and <strong>$100 received in ten years</strong> is worth
      only about <strong>$74.41</strong> in today's money. Over the decade, prices rise a total
      of about <strong>34%</strong> — that is the compounding of 3% for ten years, not simply
      3 × 10.
    </p>

    <h2>The rule of 72</h2>
    <p>
      For a quick mental estimate, divide 72 by the inflation rate to see how many years it
      takes prices to <em>double</em>. At 3%, that is 72 ÷ 3 = about 24 years; at 6% it halves
      to 12. The same shortcut works for investment growth in reverse — which is exactly why
      your returns need to clear the inflation rate to make real progress.
    </p>

    <h2>What rate should you use?</h2>
    <p>
      Long-run inflation has averaged roughly <strong>2–3% a year</strong> in many developed
      economies, and central banks such as the U.S. Federal Reserve target around 2%. Individual
      years swing well above or below that, and prices spiked far higher in some recent years, so
      treat any single rate as a scenario rather than a forecast. Running the same amount at 2%,
      3% and 5% quickly shows how sensitive long horizons are to the rate you pick.
    </p>

    <h2>Protecting your buying power</h2>
    <p>
      Money that does not grow at least as fast as inflation quietly loses value. At 3%, cash
      left idle loses roughly a quarter of its buying power in a decade, which is why savings and
      investments generally need to earn a return above the inflation rate to make real progress.
      Model that growth with the <a href="/finance/investment-calculator">investment
      calculator</a> and the <a href="/finance/compound-interest-calculator">compound interest
      calculator</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations to keep in mind</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Constant-rate assumption.</strong> The tool applies one steady rate; real inflation varies year to year and differs by category — housing, food and energy rarely move together.</li>
      <li><strong>A general index, not your basket.</strong> Published inflation tracks a broad basket of goods; your personal inflation rate depends on what you actually buy.</li>
      <li><strong>Before taxes and returns.</strong> It measures price change only, not the growth of any money you invest to keep pace.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This is a simplified model using a constant rate; real inflation varies year to year. This
      is general educational information, not financial advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
